1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,562
A recipe card in Diya's
kitchen says: sugar, two.

2
00:00:03,862 --> 00:00:04,827
Two spoons?

3
00:00:04,827 --> 00:00:05,727
Two cups?

4
00:00:05,687 --> 00:00:06,809
Two kilograms?

5
00:00:06,809 --> 00:00:09,011
The number alone means nothing.

6
00:00:09,511 --> 00:00:10,775
It needs a unit.

7
00:00:10,775 --> 00:00:15,007
And that is the first big idea in
physics: the physical quantity.

8
00:00:15,657 --> 00:00:17,740
So what is a physical quantity?

9
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:20,815
It is anything in nature
that we can measure.

10
00:00:20,815 --> 00:00:23,415
Length, mass, time, temperature, speed.

11
00:00:23,765 --> 00:00:27,625
Kindness and beauty matter, but
they are not physical quantities.

12
00:00:27,625 --> 00:00:29,556
No instrument can measure them.

13
00:00:30,156 --> 00:00:32,193
Every measurement has two parts.

14
00:00:32,193 --> 00:00:34,277
A number, called the magnitude.

15
00:00:34,277 --> 00:00:35,205
And a unit.

16
00:00:35,555 --> 00:00:40,071
A unit is a fixed amount that everyone
agrees to compare with, like one

17
00:00:40,071 --> 00:00:41,652
metre or one kilogram.

18
00:00:42,302 --> 00:00:45,097
Let's measure one pencil
on our measuring bench.

19
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:48,297
With a centimetre scale, it reads fifteen.

20
00:00:48,297 --> 00:00:49,658
Fifteen centimetres.

21
00:00:50,058 --> 00:00:51,478
Switch to millimetres.

22
00:00:51,478 --> 00:00:53,724
Now it reads one hundred and fifty.

23
00:00:54,124 --> 00:00:55,407
Switch to metres.

24
00:00:55,407 --> 00:00:57,891
Now it is only zero point one five.

25
00:00:58,291 --> 00:00:59,839
The pencil never changed.

26
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:02,259
The number and the unit changed together.

27
00:01:02,759 --> 00:01:04,037
Look at the graph.

28
00:01:04,037 --> 00:01:08,624
Each time the unit gets ten times bigger,
the number gets ten times smaller.

29
00:01:09,274 --> 00:01:12,099
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

30
00:01:12,449 --> 00:01:13,411
Step one.

31
00:01:13,411 --> 00:01:17,586
A quantity Q equals its
number n, times its unit u.

32
00:01:17,586 --> 00:01:18,922
Q equals n u.

33
00:01:19,672 --> 00:01:20,572
Step two.

34
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:24,562
The same quantity in two
different units does not change.

35
00:01:24,562 --> 00:01:27,321
So n one u one equals n two u two.

36
00:01:28,071 --> 00:01:29,031
Step three.

37
00:01:29,031 --> 00:01:33,822
The product n times u is fixed, so
n is inversely proportional to u.

38
00:01:33,822 --> 00:01:36,452
A bigger unit means a smaller number.

39
00:01:37,202 --> 00:01:40,538
On the bench, the area
of each box is n times u.

40
00:01:40,538 --> 00:01:43,654
The shape changes, but
the area stays the same.

41
00:01:44,304 --> 00:01:45,328
Remember this.

42
00:01:45,328 --> 00:01:49,959
A physical quantity is always a number
with a unit, never a number alone.

43
00:01:50,659 --> 00:01:53,879
Now, physicists sort
quantities in two big ways.

44
00:01:54,229 --> 00:01:57,547
The first way: base quantities
and derived quantities.

45
00:01:57,897 --> 00:02:00,335
Base quantities are the building blocks.

46
00:02:00,335 --> 00:02:02,455
The SI system has seven of them.

47
00:02:02,805 --> 00:02:07,286
Length, mass, time, electric current,
temperature, amount of substance,

48
00:02:07,286 --> 00:02:08,779
and luminous intensity.

49
00:02:09,179 --> 00:02:11,705
Derived quantities are built from these.

50
00:02:11,705 --> 00:02:13,591
Area is length times length.

51
00:02:13,941 --> 00:02:16,311
Speed is length divided by time.

52
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:18,745
So its unit is metre per second.

53
00:02:19,395 --> 00:02:21,714
The second way: scalars and vectors.

54
00:02:22,064 --> 00:02:23,963
A scalar has only a size.

55
00:02:23,963 --> 00:02:26,678
Mass, time and temperature are scalars.

56
00:02:27,028 --> 00:02:29,542
A vector has a size and a direction.

57
00:02:29,542 --> 00:02:32,485
Displacement, velocity
and force are vectors.

58
00:02:32,835 --> 00:02:35,345
Push a box with ten newtons to the right.

59
00:02:35,345 --> 00:02:36,388
It moves right.

60
00:02:36,738 --> 00:02:39,805
Push it with the same
ten newtons to the left.

61
00:02:39,805 --> 00:02:40,906
It moves left.

62
00:02:40,906 --> 00:02:42,843
Same size, different result.

63
00:02:42,843 --> 00:02:44,693
So force needs a direction.

64
00:02:45,343 --> 00:02:47,614
Let's sort six quantities on the board.

65
00:02:47,964 --> 00:02:50,355
Mass is a base quantity, and a scalar.

66
00:02:50,705 --> 00:02:52,787
Time is also base, and scalar.

67
00:02:53,137 --> 00:02:55,843
Speed and density are derived, and scalar.

68
00:02:56,193 --> 00:02:59,571
Velocity and force are
derived, and they are vectors.

69
00:02:59,922 --> 00:03:01,825
Notice one box stays empty.

70
00:03:01,825 --> 00:03:04,920
None of the seven base
quantities is a vector.

71
00:03:05,570 --> 00:03:07,305
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:07,655 --> 00:03:10,510
A steel rod is two point five metres long.

73
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:12,594
How long is it in centimetres?

74
00:03:13,094 --> 00:03:14,295
Write the rule.

75
00:03:14,295 --> 00:03:17,876
n two equals n one times
u one, divided by u two.

76
00:03:18,626 --> 00:03:20,646
Here n one is two point five.

77
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:22,076
u one is one metre.

78
00:03:22,076 --> 00:03:23,743
u two is one centimetre.

79
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:27,252
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

80
00:03:27,252 --> 00:03:30,729
So n two is two point
five times one hundred.

81
00:03:31,479 --> 00:03:33,429
That is two hundred and fifty.

82
00:03:33,429 --> 00:03:36,471
The rod is two hundred and
fifty centimetres long.

83
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:38,584
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:38,934 --> 00:03:43,858
Many students say electric current is a
vector, because it flows in a direction.

85
00:03:44,208 --> 00:03:45,900
But current is a scalar.

86
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:50,224
At a junction, two amperes and three
amperes always make five amperes,

87
00:03:50,224 --> 00:03:51,559
whatever the angle.

88
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:53,551
Vectors do not add like that.

89
00:03:54,201 --> 00:03:55,637
Where is this asked?

90
00:03:55,637 --> 00:03:59,994
Mostly as quick one mark questions:
spot the base quantity, the derived

91
00:03:59,994 --> 00:04:01,719
quantity, or the vector.

92
00:04:02,319 --> 00:04:03,244
Your turn.

93
00:04:03,244 --> 00:04:07,335
Which set has only derived
quantities that are also scalars?

94
00:04:07,335 --> 00:04:08,642
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:14,042 --> 00:04:15,395
The answer is A.

96
00:04:15,395 --> 00:04:17,272
Speed, density and work.

97
00:04:17,772 --> 00:04:21,174
Set B has mass and time,
which are base quantities.

98
00:04:21,174 --> 00:04:22,923
And velocity is a vector.

99
00:04:23,423 --> 00:04:25,560
Set C has force, which is a vector.

100
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:28,954
Set D has electric
current and temperature.

101
00:04:28,954 --> 00:04:30,740
Both are base quantities.

102
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:34,134
Only set A is fully
derived, and fully scalar.

103
00:04:34,734 --> 00:04:36,429
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:36,429 --> 00:04:37,734
Q equals n u.

105
00:04:37,734 --> 00:04:39,784
The seven base quantities.

106
00:04:39,784 --> 00:04:41,891
And scalars versus vectors.

107
00:04:45,091 --> 00:04:49,130
Next time, we look closer at
fundamental and derived quantities.
